; Woven DNS, for import into Cloudflare
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Cloudflare: your zone > DNS > Records > Import and Export > Import.
; Everything arrives DNS-only (grey cloud), which is what we want. See
; docs/dns/README.md for why, and for what to do after.
;
; The four domains are already registered against their Railway services and
; are waiting on these records; the CNAME targets below came from Railway's
; API, not from a guess. The mail records came from Resend's API for this
; domain, not from their generic documentation.
;
; ONE RECORD MAY NOT IMPORT: the apex CNAME.
; A zone is not allowed to hold a CNAME beside other data, and the apex here
; also carries MX and TXT. Cloudflare permits this because it flattens the
; apex CNAME into A records before publishing, so the zone the world sees has
; no conflict — but their import parser reads BIND format and may reject the
; line on principle. If the import reports an error or the bare domain is
; missing afterwards, add it by hand: Add record > CNAME > name `@` >
; target `8yqpl9im.up.railway.app` > proxy OFF. Everything else imports as is.
;
; TTL 300 while things are moving. Raise it to 3600 once the addresses have
; not changed for a week.

$ORIGIN woventechnology.com.
$TTL 300

; --- the site ---------------------------------------------------------------
; Cloudflare flattens a CNAME at the apex, so the bare domain points at Railway
; without an A record whose address we would then have to chase.
@                       300  IN  CNAME  8yqpl9im.up.railway.app.
www                     300  IN  CNAME  w4um6z2s.up.railway.app.

; --- the public API ---------------------------------------------------------
; Reservations, applications and contact. Holds no household data.
api                     300  IN  CNAME  yxc9ldh5.up.railway.app.

; --- the relay --------------------------------------------------------------
; Carries sealed frames between a Core and its dashboards and keeps nothing.
; It must stay DNS-only: the connection is a long-lived WebSocket, and a proxy
; in front of it buys nothing and can time it out mid-session.
relay                   300  IN  CNAME  pnfom1f8.up.railway.app.

; --- the demonstration house ----------------------------------------------
; A hosted Core running an example household anyone may walk through, wiped
; and reseeded on every start. It must be a subdomain of the site: the
; dashboard at woventechnology.com sends the session cookie only to the same
; site, and Safari sends it nowhere else at all.
demo                    300  IN  CNAME  2wbh1dec.up.railway.app.

; --- proving the domain is ours ---------------------------------------------
; Railway's ownership check. It can usually verify from the CNAME alone, but
; these cost nothing and remove the second round trip if it cannot. They can be
; deleted once every certificate is issued.
_railway-verify         300  IN  TXT    "railway-verify=75f21c2d317ee4e7012b619edb13874dcee700da5904715eb08fc96f9a4eff66"
_railway-verify.www     300  IN  TXT    "railway-verify=040477270e4cbf16fda366bf7aeb666b317468ef9304735bff50e600f8487bb8"
_railway-verify.api     300  IN  TXT    "railway-verify=2872d44aa0a8d33ae798b65d81c67999fafc43715b6850594a902d4b92798ab3"
_railway-verify.relay   300  IN  TXT    "railway-verify=df92e3f8d1ecbcc41a96ee7c70b5e72495ba9dc687bb430e5b143e5b72cb1033"

; --- mail -------------------------------------------------------------------
; No mail is *accepted* at this domain. A null MX says so outright, which stops
; the backscatter an unconfigured domain attracts. Sending is unaffected: it
; happens through the `send` subdomain below.
@                       300  IN  MX     0 .

; Resend sends with its own return path at send.woventechnology.com, so these
; two records are what SPF is actually checked against. Both came from Resend's
; API for this domain, not from their generic documentation.
send                    300  IN  MX     10 feedback-smtp.us-east-1.amazonses.com.
send                    300  IN  TXT    "v=spf1 include:amazonses.com ~all"

; The apex keeps its hard fail. Nothing legitimately sends with the bare domain
; as the envelope sender — Resend uses `send` above — so anything that claims to
; is forged. If Resend ever reports an SPF failure, this record is the first
; thing to look at, but it should not need touching.
@                       300  IN  TXT    "v=spf1 -all"

; DKIM. Resend signs as d=woventechnology.com, so this is what makes DMARC pass
; under strict alignment. One long value on one line; if your editor wraps it,
; the record is broken.
resend._domainkey       300  IN  TXT    "p=MIGfMA0GCSqGSIb3DQEBAQUAA4GNADCBiQKBgQDgxuSSqWXYYRw9CgyPKJPwhJ+SwPKcQNN4Exhatjo1aEClTybX3b4VPD9mlJ5WFpevRy2X04h/uIVSMH4NBFsNuPEtvpPQw0888WbWInEa36py1BC7IJNEDNz2ApCm5VGRZml/q7zFIDktKffdZU1Rg13n4gWrVJgoAD90Yr22oQIDAQAB"

; Reject anything that fails both checks.
;
; aspf is relaxed, not strict: the envelope sender is send.woventechnology.com
; while the From header is woventechnology.com, and strict alignment would call
; that a mismatch. DKIM stays strict because Resend signs as the apex exactly.
;
; There is no rua. Reports would be sent to a mailbox at a domain whose MX says
; it accepts no mail, and pointing them at an address elsewhere needs a record
; on that other domain authorising it. A rua nobody can read is worse than none,
; because it looks like the reports are being watched. Add one when there is a
; mailbox to read it in.
_dmarc                  300  IN  TXT    "v=DMARC1; p=reject; aspf=r; adkim=s"

; --- nothing else -----------------------------------------------------------
; No wildcard. A wildcard answers for every name anyone invents, which is how a
; subdomain nobody meant to publish ends up serving something.
